cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use these measurements or the numbers derived from them, please cite this repository."
title: "kv-cache-lab: VRAM accounting for KV cache quantization on consumer GPUs"
abstract: >-
  A single-script measurement of what KV cache quantization saves in VRAM on a consumer
  GPU - one RTX 4060 Laptop, one model, one load per context length, five context lengths.
  A straight-line fit gives 32.11 +/- 1.24 KiB/token for f16, but the residuals form a
  smooth arc peaking at 8192 and reject that model (F = 2748 against a 5% critical value of
  18.513). Adding a one-time 67.1 MiB allocation step at 8192 fits the same data to an SSE
  of 2.0 and moves the slope to 29.99 KiB/token. A quadratic carries the same three
  parameters and fits 486x worse, so the bend is a jump rather than curvature. The
  per-token overhead over theory is therefore 1.99 KiB rather than the 4.1 KiB a naive fit
  reports, and about half of the difference was the step being amortised over 32K tokens.
  The q8_0 run is reported as a range rather than a point (slope 16.5-17.3 KiB/token)
  because its idle VRAM baseline drifted 26 MiB during measurement; the f16 run held to
  1 MiB and is unaffected. No claim is made that the overhead differs between precisions.
  The q8_0 grid was subsequently re-measured under a different protocol - median-sampled
  idles, peak-sampled loads, unload confirmed through the Ollama API rather than a VRAM
  threshold, and the KV cache type read from the server's own environment - on a desktop
  carrying 1078 MiB more VRAM. Every one of the five deltas landed within 4 MiB of the
  original, the slope moved 19.08 to 19.17 KiB/token, and the step moved 56.2 to 59.8 MiB
  at the same location. The f16 grid was not re-run, because that would require restarting
  the server with a different OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE.
  Scope: memory only, no throughput, no output quality, no repeated measurements per grid
  point. The repository ships the measuring script, the raw CSVs, the replication CSV, and
  89 self-checks that recompute every quoted number without a GPU.
